  <section>
    <title>Additional Resources</title>

    <section>
      <title>Solving Systems by Graphing</title>
      <para>The graph of a linear equation is a line. Each point on the line is a solution to the
        equation. For a system of two equations, we will graph two lines. Then we can see all the
        points that are solutions to each equation. And, by finding what the lines have in common,
        we’ll find the solution to the system.</para>
      <para>Most linear equations in one variable have one solution, but we saw that some equations,
        called contradictions, have no solutions and for other equations, called identities, all
        numbers are solutions.</para>
      <para>
        <figure class="unnumbered">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_01.jpg" />
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para>
        <emphasis>Part 1:Writing a System of Equations from a Given Graph</emphasis>
      </para>
      <note>
        <para>How to write a system of linear equations from graphs.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first line.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second line.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis>
        - Write the two equations for a system of equations.</para>
      </note>
      <para>
        <emphasis>Example 1</emphasis>
      </para>
      <para>Write a system of equations from the graph.</para>
      <para>
        <figure class="unnumbered">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_02.jpg" />
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
        \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}r}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
        \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\mathrm{\style{color:red}o}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
        line.</para>
      <para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(0-(-3))}{(6-0)}=\frac36=\frac12\)</para>
      <para>\(y\)-intercept = \((0, -3)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
        \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{blue}}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{solid}}\) line.</para>
      <para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(3-(-3))}{(3-0)}=\frac63=\frac21\)</para>
      <para>\(y\)-intercept = \((0, -3)\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the two equations for a system of equations.</para>
      <para>\(y = mx+b\)</para>
      <para>
        \({\style{color:red}y} {\style{color:red}=}\frac
        {\style{color:red}1}{\style{color:red}2}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}3}\)</para>
      <para>\({\style{color:blue}y} {\style{color:blue}=}
        {\style{color:blue}2}{\style{color:blue}x}{\style{color:blue}-}{\style{color:blue}3}\)</para>
      <para>
        <emphasis>Part 2:Use a Graph to Solve a Given System</emphasis>
      </para>
      <note>
        <para>How to solve a system of linear equations by graphing.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis>
        - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis>
        - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate system.</para>
        <para><emphasis>Step
        4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
      </note>
      <para>If the lines intersect, estimate the point of intersection. Check to make sure it is a
        solution to both equations. This is the solution to the system.</para>
      <list>
        <item>If the lines are parallel, the system has no solution.</item>
        <item>If the lines are the same, the system has an infinite number of solutions.</item>
      </list>
      <para>
        <emphasis>Example 2</emphasis>
      </para>
      <para>Solve the system by graphing:</para>
      <para>\(y = 2x+1\)</para>
      <para>\(y =4x-1\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and /(y/)-intercept of the first equation.</para>
      <para>In \(y=2x+1\), \(m=2\), \(b=1\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second equation.</para>
      <para>In \(y=4x-1\), \(m=4\), \(b= -1\)</para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular coordinate
        system.</para>
      <para>
        <figure class="unnumbered">
          <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_03.jpg" />
          </media>
        </figure>
      </para>
      <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
      <para>The point where the lines intersect is:</para>
      <para>\((1, 3)\)</para>
      <para>Check to make sure the estimated point, \((1, 3)\) solves the system by substituting the
        values for \(x\) and \(y\) into both equations.</para>
      <para>Check the first equation: \(𝑦=2𝑥+1\)</para>
      <para>\(x = 1\)</para>
      <para>\(y = 3\)</para>
      <para>\(y = 2x + 1\)</para>
      <para>\((3) = 2(1) + 1\)</para>
      <para>\(3 = 2 + 1\)</para>
      <para>\(3 = 3\)</para>
      <para>So, \((1, 3)\) solves \(y = 2x + 1\)</para>
      <para>Check the second equation: \(𝑦=4𝑥−1\)</para>
      <para>\(y = 4x - 1\)</para>
      <para>\((3) = 4(1) - 1\)</para>
      <para>\(3 = 4 - 1\)</para>
      <para>\(3 = 3\)</para>
      <para>So, \((1, 3)\) solves \(y = 4x - 1\)</para>

      <note class="try look-at-me">
      <title>Try It: Solving Systems by Graphing</title>
      <!--Q#1-->
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Write a system of equations from the graph.</para>
            <para>
              <figure class="unnumbered">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_04.jpg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Here is how to solve the system by graphing:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
              \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}r}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
              \(\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\mathrm{\style{color:red}o}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}t}\mathrm{\style{color:red}e}\mathrm{\style{color:red}d}\)
              line.</para>
            <para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac
              {(0-(1))}{(2-0)}=\frac{-1}{2}=\frac{-1}{2}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the
              \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{blue}}\) \(\mathrm{\style{color:blue}{solid}}\) line.</para>
            <para>\(m =\frac {(y_1-y_2)}{(x_1-x_2)}=\frac {(1-(-3))}{(1-0)}=\frac{4}{1}\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Write the two equations for a system of equation.</para>
            <para>
              \({\style{color:red}y} {\style{color:red}=}\frac
              {{\style{color:red}-}{\style{color:red}1}}{{\style{color:red}2}}{\style{color:red}x}{\style{color:red}+}{\style{color:red}1}\)</para>
            <para>\({\style{color:blue}y} {\style{color:blue}=}
              {\style{color:blue}4}{\style{color:blue}x}{\style{color:blue}-}{\style{color:blue}3}\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <!--Interaction
      End -->

        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <para>Solve the system by graphing:</para>
            <para>\(y=3x+3\)</para>
            <para>\(y= -x+7\)</para>
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
              compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <para>Here is how to solve the system by graphing:</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the first
              equation.</para>
            <para>In \(y=3x+3\), \(m=3\), \(b=3\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Find the slope and \(y\)-intercept of the second
              equation.</para>
            <para>In \(y= -x+7\), \(m=-1\), \(b= 7\)</para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Graph the two equations on the same rectangular
              coordinate system.</para>
            <para>
              <figure class="unnumbered">
                <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_08_05.jpg" />
                </media>
              </figure>
            </para>
            <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Estimate the solution to the system.</para>
            <para>\((1, 6)\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
